      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by finding the radius of the base of the original cone. The circumference of the base is given as \\(16\\pi\\) inches. The formula for the circumference of a circle is \\(C = 2\\pi r\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi \\implies r = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nFor the original cone, the height \\(h\\) is 30 inches. Substituting the values of \\(r\\) and \\(h\\) into the volume formula, we get:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\nNext, we need to determine the height of the shorter cone, which is 12 inches (since the volume of the shorter cone is given as \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches). Using the volume formula for the original cone, we solve for the radius \\(r\\) of the base of the original cone in terms of the height \\(h\\):\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 (30) \\implies 192 = 10r^2 \\implies r^2 = \\frac{192}{10} = 19.2 \\implies r = \\sqrt{19.2} = \\sqrt{\\frac{48}{2.5}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{96}{5}} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{30}}{5}\n\\]\nNow, we use the same radius for the shorter cone to find its height. The volume formula again gives us:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{4\\sqrt{30}}{5}\\right)^2 h = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{16 \\cdot 30}{25}\\right) h = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\left(\\frac{480}{25}\\right) h = \\frac{16\\pi h}{5}\n\\]\nSolving for \\(h\\), we get:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{16\\pi h}{5} \\implies 192 \\cdot 5 = 16h \\implies 960 = 16h \\implies h = \\frac{960}{16} = 60 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\frac{12}{30} = \\frac{2}{5}\n\\]\nThus, the ratio is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{5}}\n\\]",
