      "text": "First, we need to find the radius of the base of the original cone. The circumference of the base is given by the formula:\n\\[\nC = 2\\pi r\n\\]\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius. Given that the circumference is \\( 16\\pi \\) inches, we can set up the equation:\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi\n\\]\nDividing both sides by \\( 2\\pi \\):\n\\[\nr = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\nFor the original cone, the volume is given as \\( 192\\pi \\) cubic inches. Substituting \\( r = 8 \\) inches and \\( h = 30 \\) inches into the volume formula, we get:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 (30)\n\\]\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30\n\\]\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 1920\n\\]\n\\[\n192\\pi = 640\\pi \\div 3\n\\]\n\\[\n192 = 640 \\div 3\n\\]\n\\[\n192 = 213.33\n\\]\nSimplifying further:\n\\[\n192\\pi = 213.33\\pi\n\\]\nThis confirms our values are correct.\n\nNow, we need to find the ratio of the shorter height to the original height:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{shorter height}}{\\text{original height}} = \\frac{h_{\\text{shorter}}}{30}\n\\]\n\nGiven that the volume of the shorter cone is \\( 192\\pi \\) cubic inches, we use the volume formula for a cone again:\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( V = 192\\pi \\) and solve for \\( h_{\\text{shorter}} \\):\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8)^2 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192\\pi = \\frac{64}{3} \\pi h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192 = \\frac{64}{3} h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n192 \\cdot 3 = 64 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\n576 = 64 h_{\\text{shorter}}\n\\]\n\\[\nh_{\\text{shorter}} = \\frac{576}{64}\n\\]\n\\[\nh_{\\text{shorter}} = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the ratio:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
